enters a `while not self.check()` loop and calls `time.sleep(window - elapsed)`. With `burst_size=2`
and two records at ~the same instant, `elapsed ≈ 0`, so the guard will sleep for ~`window_seconds`
(1.0s) of real wall-clock time before returning. This makes the test (a) unnecessarily slow (~1s per
run) and (b) flaky: `time.sleep` can return slightly early and `check()` removes a timestamp only
when `now - t >= window` (strict `<` comparison in `_cleanup_and_get_limits`), so any clock jitter
or early wakeup causes an extra loop iteration or even a transiently wrong `waited`/return value.
Unit tests for rate limiting should inject a fake clock / mock `time.time` and `time.sleep` instead
of relying on real-time behavior.

